Hold the waiter's mutex when signalling BatchSpanProcessor, so wakeups are not lost.

Every condition_variable in BatchSpanProcessor is notified without holding the
mutex its waiter evaluates the predicate under. A notification issued while the
waiter is still inside its predicate -- after the predicate read the state but
before the waiter has atomically released the mutex and registered as a waiter
-- reaches no waiter and is lost. The waiter then sleeps out its full timeout.
Both directions of the force-flush handshake are affected:

1. Worker wakeup (`cv` / `cv_m`). ForceFlush's break_condition and
   InternalShutdown both set is_force_wakeup_background_worker and call
   cv.notify_all() without cv_m, while DoBackgroundWork evaluates its predicate
   under cv_m. A lost wakeup here makes the worker sleep its whole
   schedule_delay_millis (5s by default).

2. Flush completion (`force_flush_cv` / `force_flush_cv_m`). NotifyCompletion
   publishes force_flush_notified_sequence and calls force_flush_cv.notify_all()
   without force_flush_cv_m, while ForceFlush reads that same sequence from its
   predicate under force_flush_cv_m. A lost wakeup here makes ForceFlush sleep
   its whole wait quantum, which is also schedule_delay_millis.

Direction 1 is the damaging one, because it does not merely delay ForceFlush --
it makes it return the wrong answer. ForceFlush's retry loop is calibrated so it
never gets a second iteration: the inner wait quantum is schedule_delay_millis_
(5s) and callers typically pass a 5s timeout, so break_condition runs exactly
once and the caller's deadline expires at the same instant the worker finally
times out and exports. ForceFlush returns false having exported nothing, and the
caller sees an empty batch even though the spans were queued.

That is the intermittent failure this was tracked down from: scada's
OtelTraceSinkTest ends spans, calls ForceFlush(5s) and asserts on the batch, and
sporadically saw zero spans with the test taking ~5005ms instead of ~0ms.
Measured on macOS (2026-07-25), counting runs of that suite where some test took
>= 1000ms: 19/100 stalled unpatched, 14/200 with only direction 1 fixed, and
0/200 with both fixed. The residual direction-2 stalls all still *passed* -- a
correct result delivered 5s late -- which is why fixing only direction 1 turns
the failures into slow passes rather than eliminating the stall.

The fix takes the waiter's mutex around each store-and-notify. Doing that at all
four sites requires one supporting change, because the naive version deadlocks:
break_condition runs under force_flush_cv_m and now takes cv_m, so the reverse
edge cv_m -> force_flush_cv_m must not exist. It otherwise would, since the
worker held cv_m across Export() -> NotifyCompletion(). DoBackgroundWork
therefore releases cv_m once the predicate has been consumed, before
Export()/DrainQueue(). Nothing below that point needs cv_m: it guards only the
wait predicate, whose inputs (the is_* flags and the lock-free buffer_) are
atomic. That leaves a strict order with no cycle:

    force_flush_cv_m -> cv_m   (ForceFlush's break_condition)
    shutdown_m       -> cv_m   (InternalShutdown)
    force_flush_cv_m           (NotifyCompletion, holding nothing else)

NotifyCompletion deliberately does not hold force_flush_cv_m across
exporter->ForceFlush(), only across publishing the sequence and notifying.

The worker's wait predicate additionally now checks is_shutdown, which it never
did, even though the loop body handles is_shutdown immediately after the wait --
the worker relied entirely on is_force_wakeup_background_worker being observed.
With the notifiers holding cv_m, the worker's unconditional
is_force_wakeup_background_worker.store(false) after wait_for can also no longer
swallow a concurrent force-wakeup, since setting that flag now requires cv_m.

Unlike restore-metric-reader-shutdown-lock.patch this is not a restoration of a
reverted upstream commit, it is a new fix. Upstream v1.27.0, v1.28.0 and main
all still notify outside the lock at all four sites (checked 2026-07-25).

--- a/sdk/src/trace/batch_span_processor.cc
+++ b/sdk/src/trace/batch_span_processor.cc
@@ -129,6 +129,8 @@
     if (synchronization_data_->force_flush_pending_sequence.load(std::memory_order_acquire) >
         synchronization_data_->force_flush_notified_sequence.load(std::memory_order_acquire))
     {
+      // ensure that `cv` is awaiting, and the update doesn't get lost
+      std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(synchronization_data_->cv_m);
       synchronization_data_->is_force_wakeup_background_worker.store(true,
                                                                      std::memory_order_release);
       synchronization_data_->cv.notify_all();
@@ -194,6 +196,11 @@
     std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(synchronization_data_->cv_m);
     synchronization_data_->cv.wait_for(lk, timeout, [this] {
       if (synchronization_data_->is_force_wakeup_background_worker.load(std::memory_order_acquire))
+      {
+        return true;
+      }
+
+      if (synchronization_data_->is_shutdown.load(std::memory_order_acquire))
       {
         return true;
       }
@@ -203,6 +210,13 @@
     synchronization_data_->is_force_wakeup_background_worker.store(false,
                                                                    std::memory_order_release);
 
+    // Release cv_m before Export()/DrainQueue() below. NotifyCompletion() takes
+    // force_flush_cv_m, and ForceFlush's break_condition takes cv_m while holding
+    // force_flush_cv_m; holding cv_m across the export would invert that order.
+    // Nothing below needs cv_m: it guards only the wait predicate, whose inputs
+    // (the is_* flags and the lock-free buffer_) are already atomic.
+    lk.unlock();
+
 #ifdef ENABLE_THREAD_INSTRUMENTATION_PREVIEW
     if (worker_thread_instrumentation_ != nullptr)
     {
@@ -311,6 +325,12 @@
       exporter->ForceFlush(timeout);
     }
 
+    // ensure that `force_flush_cv` is awaiting, and the update doesn't get lost.
+    // The waiter in ForceFlush reads force_flush_notified_sequence from its wait
+    // predicate under force_flush_cv_m, so publishing the new sequence and
+    // notifying must be mutually exclusive with that read. Deliberately does not
+    // cover exporter->ForceFlush() above, which may block for a long time.
+    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(synchronization_data->force_flush_cv_m);
     std::uint64_t notified_sequence =
         synchronization_data->force_flush_notified_sequence.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
     while (notify_force_flush > notified_sequence)
@@ -378,8 +398,13 @@
 
   if (worker_thread_.joinable())
   {
-    synchronization_data_->is_force_wakeup_background_worker.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
-    synchronization_data_->cv.notify_all();
+    {
+      // ensure that `cv` is awaiting, and the update doesn't get lost
+      std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(synchronization_data_->cv_m);
+      synchronization_data_->is_force_wakeup_background_worker.store(true,
+                                                                     std::memory_order_release);
+      synchronization_data_->cv.notify_all();
+    }
     worker_thread_.join();
   }
 
